1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
 Alright guys, you're nearly there with this linearization business, right?

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,520
 We've talked about linearizing kind of 1D functions.

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:10,960
 We've talked about linearizing multivariable functions with multiple inputs and one output.

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,760
 We've talked about linearizing state space models, right?

5
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:18,680
 Which are just kind of collections of multivariable functions.

6
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:21,840
 And now we're going to talk about doing the same for state space models with inputs,

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,920
 a really minor extension on what we've already done.

8
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:29,120
 You remember from the last video that our linearization formula for a state space equation

9
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:33,080
 looked like kind of an offset term, which was the value of that equation evaluated

10
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:36,640
 at our approximation point, kind of our approximation z.

11
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:40,680
 Plus, a gradient times a distance for each of the states involved.

12
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:44,560
 Well, if you want to linearize a state space model that has inputs as well as states,

13
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:50,240
 so kind of z1 through zn and u1 through un, it's exactly the same process.

14
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:54,920
 You have an offset term plus a gradient times a distance for each state from the approximation.

15
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:58,520
 Plus, a gradient times a distance for each input.

16
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:04,040
 What this means is that our evaluation for the offset term at the start now needs to be in terms of,

17
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:08,280
 kind of, a state approximation point and an input approximation point.

18
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:13,560
 And that we need to calculate distances not just from each state to the kind of state approximation.

19
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:16,920
 But also from each input to the input approximation.

20
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:23,200
 But really, this just shows up as kind of another term for each input in the same way that we had a term for each state.

21
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,800
 A approximating state space models with inputs is basically the same procedure.

22
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:29,520
 And it's why I haven't made a big fuss about it until now.

23
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:32,240
 In the next video, we're going to work through a problem when we do that.

24
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,600
 But there's one more thing I want to talk about in this video.

25
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:40,560
 And that is writing our linear approximations with all of our inputs in matrix form.

26
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:42,800
 You'll remember back all the way to week two or week three,

27
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:46,520
 where I talked about how we take a linear state space model and write it in matrix form,

28
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:52,600
 kind of a z. equals a z plus b u and y equals c z plus d u.

29
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,960
 We can do exactly the same thing for a linearization of a state space model.

30
00:01:55,960 --> 00:02:01,280
 Since our linearization is by definition a linear state space model.

31
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:10,880
 When you do this, the kind of matrices, the ABCD matrices of your linearization become really important in doing some analysis on what we call equilibrium points a bit later on.

32
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:14,240
 The topic of the video series that comes immediately after this one.

33
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:19,000
 So all I want to say to you at this point is make sure that when you linearize your state space models,

34
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,920
 you also calculate how you would write that in matrix form.

35
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:26,680
 Because nine times out of 10, you're going to want to use those matrices to do some further analysis.

36
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,600
 One more kind of quick note at the end of all of this.

37
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:34,720
 You'll notice that I haven't talked about what you do with output equations when you're linearizing.

38
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:38,520
 And that's because you do exactly the same thing as with your state space equations.

39
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:41,800
 You use exactly the same kind of linearization formulas.

40
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:45,560
 You just replace every instance of z something dot with y.

41
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:51,880
 It is literally exactly the same process because in the same way that a state space equation is just a multi variable function.

42
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:55,280
 And we use the multi variable kind of linearization equation.

43
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:59,040
 And output equation is itself just a multi variable function.

44
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:01,240
 And you use the same kind of equation.

45
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:02,480
 That's all I have for this one.

46
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:06,880
 Let's go and work on that problem where we're going to kind of linearize a state space model with inputs.

47
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:09,320
 And then we'll talk about equilibrium points and stability.

48
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:12,080
 The last two topics of the modeling section of this course.
